From ae7f00d03b1847dcd34c423ba392d8ea2d4462af Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Luis Henrique Silva Cazuriaga
 <LuisHenrique.SilvaCazuriaga@windriver.com>
Date: Wed, 9 Sep 2026 11:41:06 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] Fix cold migration on IPv6 deployments

VM cold migration fails on IPv6 deployments because the nova
compute init and SSH start scripts use hardcoded IPv4 commands
(ip -4) to determine the migration interface and SSH listen
address. On pure IPv6 deployments, these commands fail to find
any interface, causing the SSH daemon to fall back to listening
on 127.0.0.1, making it unreachable from other compute nodes.

Fix by detecting whether the configured subnet is IPv4 or IPv6
based on the presence of ":" in the address, then using the
appropriate ip -4/ip -6 command and inet/inet6 grep keyword
accordingly. When no subnet is configured, try IPv4 first and
fall back to IPv6. Also add scope global filter to avoid
selecting link-local addresses on IPv6 deployments.

Also avoid overwriting IPv6 addresses derived from
address_search_enabled with the IPv4 CLUSTER_HOST_IP value in
/tmp/pod-shared/nova.conf. This preserves the existing
CLUSTER_HOST_IP-based behavior for deployments that do not
enable address_search while allowing the explicit IPv6
address-search configs to take precedence when enabled.

When an explicit subnet is configured but no matching route is
present, fall back to locating an interface that already owns a
global address in that subnet. This keeps simplex labs working
when the subnet is locally attached but not returned by
`ip route list <cidr>`.

If the subnet still cannot be resolved inside the container,
fall back to the first global address of the selected IP family.
This preserves the legacy simplex behavior instead of failing
the SSH sidecar startup.

Change-Id: Ia515c6b4c08147f510cec0df6348881a51210e9a
Signed-off-by: Luis Henrique Silva Cazuriaga <LuisHenrique.SilvaCazuriaga@windriver.com>

[ Adapt OSH patches to Gazpacho release ]
Signed-off-by: Daniel Caires <DanielMarques.Caires@windriver.com>

[ Fix address selection when mgmt and cluster-host share a port ]

The interface-based address selection above still has a gap: once
the right interface is resolved, it takes the first global address
on that interface. When a single NIC hosts more than one network
(e.g. mgmt and cluster-host on the same port), that first address
can belong to the wrong network, so sshd and live migration end up
using the management address instead of the cluster-host one:

  ssh: connect to host <cluster-host-ip> port 8022: Connection refused

Add address_in_subnet(), which asks the kernel for the address that
actually belongs to the configured subnet ("ip addr show to
<subnet>") instead of relying on address ordering. The search is
scoped to the resolved interface first, then widened to all
interfaces, then retried without the scope global restriction, since
on AIO-SX the cluster-host address can live on loopback with scope
host rather than scope global.

This is used for the SSH listen address, live_migration_inbound_addr
and my_ip, guarded by is_wildcard_subnet() so that wildcard subnets
(0/0, 0.0.0.0/0, ::/0) keep the existing interface-based lookup,
which also remains as a fallback when the subnet cannot be resolved.
The CIDR values are read outside the interface detection block so
this also applies when live_migration_interface or host_interface is
set explicitly.

Change-Id: Icb792eefab456bdbee84964b1e43ba4a1560726a
Signed-off-by: Luis Henrique Silva Cazuriaga <LuisHenrique.SilvaCazuriaga@windriver.com>

[ Rework migration address selection ]

Replace the address-order-dependent shell helpers with a shared selector
used by Nova and libvirt. Prefer a matching Kubernetes Node address,
validate explicit addresses and interfaces, and reject floating-address
fallbacks on configured Node networks. Retain a unique-local fallback for
dedicated networks and the legacy behavior for wildcard CIDRs.

Select Nova host, live migration, and SSH addresses once per pod, and use
one generated libvirt configuration for both daemon startup paths.
Preserve concrete Nova and libvirt overrides and generate nova.conf
atomically across container restarts.

Project status.hostIPs on Kubernetes 1.30 and newer so dual-stack
deployments can select the secondary address family, with status.hostIP
kept as the compatibility fallback.

Signed-off-by: Luis Henrique Silva Cazuriaga <LuisHenrique.SilvaCazuriaga@windriver.com>

[ Preserve address-selection compatibility during upgrades ]

Quote every selector Secret value so empty settings remain present during a
chart upgrade instead of becoming missing Secret keys. Accept the legacy
IPv4 0/0 wildcard and reject unusable IPv6 addresses represented by either
iproute2 JSON flags or booleans.

Keep an explicitly configured interface authoritative when the platform
default CIDR does not match it, while still requiring a unique usable
address on that interface.

When a matching Node address is unavailable, do not let an explicit
interface fall back to an address in the requested or platform Node
networks. Such an address may be a movable floating address. Keep the
unique-address fallback for dedicated networks, but require the requested
IP family; cross-family interface overrides must also set a matching CIDR.

Signed-off-by: Luis Henrique Silva Cazuriaga <LuisHenrique.SilvaCazuriaga@windriver.com>
